text:
The same model in Stan, and the funnel
In Stan, the model is a few lines. The obvious way to write it, the centered parameterization, is:
parameters {
  real mu;
  real&lt;lower=0&gt; tau;
  vector[J] theta;
}
model {
  tau ~ normal(0, 2);
  theta ~ normal(mu, tau);
  y ~ normal(theta, se);
}
It ran with 3 divergent transitions:

#&gt; $num_divergent
#&gt; [1] 2 0 0 1
#&gt; 
#&gt; $num_max_treedepth
#&gt; [1] 0 0 0 0
#&gt; 
#&gt; $ebfmi
#&gt; [1] 0.8328442 0.8667000 0.9018811 0.8879878

All three happened at small values of \tau, from 0.34 to 0.42, below 95% of the posterior. That’s the signature of the funnel. When \tau is small, the \theta_j are forced close to \mu, so the posterior is very narrow in their directions. When \tau is large, they spread out. A step size that suits the wide part is far too large for the neck, and the sampler diverges there, so the neck is under-explored and the draws are biased toward larger \tau. Here’s the funnel for lab E, from the exact draws:

ggplot(data.frame(log_tau = log(td), dev = th[, 5] - md), aes(x = log_tau, y = dev)) +
  geom_point(size = 0.6, alpha = 0.3, color = "#C2416C") +
  labs(x = "log τ (between-lab SD)", y = "Lab E: θ − μ (mg/kg)") +
  theme_minimal(base_size = 12)








The fix is to change the parameterization, not the model. Write \theta_j = \mu + \tau z_j, with z_j \sim N(0, 1), and let Stan sample the z_j: they have the same width whatever \tau is, so there’s no funnel. This non-centered parameterization defines exactly the same model:
parameters {
  real mu;
  real&lt;lower=0&gt; tau;
  vector[J] z;
}
transformed parameters {
  vector[J] theta = mu + tau * z;
}
model {
  tau ~ normal(0, 2);
  z ~ std_normal();
  y ~ normal(theta, se);
}
It ran with no divergences at all:

#&gt; $num_divergent
#&gt; [1] 0 0 0 0
#&gt; 
#&gt; $num_max_treedepth
#&gt; [1] 0 0 0 0
#&gt; 
#&gt; $ebfmi
#&gt; [1] 0.7611760 0.7825335 0.8440611 0.7530742

Non-centering helps when the data say little about each group, as here. When every group has lots of data, the centered version can be better. brms uses the non-centered parameterization for all its varying effects.
Your turn. The setup code doesn’t have Stan’s draws yet. Read them from data/stan_labs_draws.csv, and compare the posterior means of \mu and \tau with the exact computation’s 48.72 and 1.10.









The column is tau.


stan &lt;- read_csv("data/stan_labs_draws.csv", show_col_types = FALSE)
c(mu = mean(stan$mu), tau = mean(stan$tau), theta_E = mean(stan$`theta[5]`))
48.72, 1.11 and 48.24, against the exact 48.72, 1.10 and 48.25: the same posterior. (Column names like theta[5] need backticks in R.) For bigger hierarchical models, there’s no exact method, and Stan’s non-centered version is the way to go.

A Stan program
Here is Module 4’s pumps model in Stan: a Weibull lifetime distribution, with some pumps still running (right-censored), and Module 4’s priors. You can download it.
// Weibull lifetimes with right-censoring: Module 4's pumps, with Module 4's priors
data {
  int&lt;lower=0&gt; N;                          // number of pumps
  vector&lt;lower=0&gt;[N] days;                 // days to failure, or to the end of the study
  array[N] int&lt;lower=0, upper=1&gt; failed;   // 1 = failed, 0 = still running (censored)
}
parameters {
  real log_shape;
  real log_scale;
}
transformed parameters {
  real&lt;lower=0&gt; shape = exp(log_shape);
  real&lt;lower=0&gt; scale = exp(log_scale);
}
model {
  log_shape ~ normal(0, 1);                // priors
  log_scale ~ normal(7.5, 1);
  for (i in 1:N) {                         // likelihood
    if (failed[i] == 1) {
      target += weibull_lpdf(days[i] | shape, scale);
    } else {
      target += weibull_lccdf(days[i] | shape, scale);
    }
  }
}
generated quantities {
  real b10 = scale * pow(-log(0.9), 1 / shape);   // the B10 life, in days
}
Each block has one job:

data declares what you’ll pass in from R, with types and constraints. Stan checks them, so a negative number of days is an error, not a silent bug.
parameters declares the unknowns that Stan samples.
transformed parameters computes functions of them that the model uses, and saves them in the output.
model builds the log-posterior. x ~ normal(0, 1) adds the normal log-density to it, and target += ... adds anything you like. Here, a failed pump adds the Weibull log-density (_lpdf), and a censored one adds the log of the probability of surviving that long, the complementary CDF (_lccdf). That’s the censored likelihood from Module 4, Lesson 9, in two lines.
generated quantities computes things from each draw after sampling, such as the B10 life, or simulated data for posterior predictive checks.

Stan is a typed language, a bit like C: every variable has a declared type, statements end with semicolons, and comments start with //.

Challenge round
Challenge 1 · A spike detector. A sensor glitch is a spike: a single reading far from both of its neighbors. Find the R1 temperatures that differ by more than 100 °C from the reading before them and from the reading after them.









lead() is the reading after. Compare with it the same way as with lag().


sensor |&gt;
  filter(abs(R1_temp_C - lag(R1_temp_C)) &gt; 100,
         abs(R1_temp_C - lead(R1_temp_C)) &gt; 100) |&gt;
  select(timestamp, R1_temp_C)
Exactly one spike: the 999.9 °C at 03:15. Why 100 °C? In 5 minutes, a real reactor temperature rises by about 30 °C while heating up, and falls by up to 50 °C while cooling down after a batch, but it never changes by 100.




Challenge 2 · From raw to right. Finish the cleaning: flag the values below the detection limit, parse the values, remove the duplicates, and deal with the impossible and censored values. Then compare your table with the lab’s database, official. How many of your 124 values agree with it?









below_lod is TRUE when the text value starts with &lt;: str_detect(value, "^&lt;"). It has to be computed before value is turned into a number.


The plant’s convention for &lt;20 is half the detection limit: below_lod ~ 10.


final &lt;- lab_raw |&gt;
  mutate(below_lod = str_detect(value, "^&lt;"),
         value = parse_number(str_replace(value, ",", "."), na = c("", "NA", "n/a", "-"))) |&gt;
  distinct(batch_id, analyte, measured_at, .keep_all = TRUE) |&gt;
  mutate(value = case_when(
    analyte == "purity" & value &gt; 100 ~ NA,
    analyte == "water" & value &lt; 0 ~ NA,
    below_lod ~ 10,
    .default = value
  ))
final |&gt;
  inner_join(official, by = c("batch_id", "analyte", "measured_at"), suffix = c("", "_db")) |&gt;
  summarise(rows = n(), agree = sum(near(value, value_db), na.rm = TRUE))
118 of the 124 values agree. The other six are the four missing values and the two impossible ones, which the spreadsheet simply doesn’t have. The lab’s database has all six, straight from the instruments. That’s the best fix of all: when you can, go back to the source.

left_join(): add columns from another table
left_join(x, y) keeps every row of x, and adds the columns of y wherever the keys match. Here’s each batch’s lot, with the lot’s supplier and delivery date added on:

batches |&gt;
  select(batch_id, lot_id) |&gt;
  left_join(lots, by = "lot_id") |&gt;
  head(4)
#&gt;   batch_id  lot_id supplier_id   received assay_pct
#&gt; 1      B01 L26-001          S1 2026-02-02     98.55
#&gt; 2      B02 L26-001          S1 2026-02-02     98.55
#&gt; 3      B03 L26-001          S1 2026-02-02     98.55
#&gt; 4      B04 L26-001          S1 2026-02-02     98.55

by = "lot_id" names the key. You can chain joins, and add the supplier’s name too:

batches |&gt;
  select(batch_id, lot_id) |&gt;
  left_join(lots, by = "lot_id") |&gt;
  left_join(suppliers, by = "supplier_id") |&gt;
  select(batch_id, lot_id, name, country) |&gt;
  head(4)
#&gt;   batch_id  lot_id               name country
#&gt; 1      B01 L26-001 Fizzwell Chemicals     USA
#&gt; 2      B02 L26-001 Fizzwell Chemicals     USA
#&gt; 3      B03 L26-001 Fizzwell Chemicals     USA
#&gt; 4      B04 L26-001 Fizzwell Chemicals     USA

Every batch kept its row and gained columns. That’s a mutating join: it changes the table by adding columns.
Your turn. Add the operators’ names to the batches, then count how many batches each operator ran.









Both tables have an operator_id column. That’s the key.


batches |&gt;
  left_join(operators, by = "operator_id") |&gt;
  count(name)
Maya ran the most batches (11), and Sam the fewest (3).

Solving the water mystery
Now for the question we started with. The path goes through four tables: water results → batches (for the lot) → lots (for the supplier) → suppliers (for the name).
Your turn. Complete the chain of joins, then compute the mean water content for each supplier.









batches links to lots by "lot_id", and lots links to suppliers by "supplier_id".


lab |&gt;
  filter(analyte == "water") |&gt;
  select(batch_id, water_ppm = value) |&gt;
  left_join(batches, by = "batch_id") |&gt;
  left_join(lots, by = "lot_id") |&gt;
  left_join(suppliers, by = "supplier_id") |&gt;
  group_by(name) |&gt;
  summarise(n = n(), mean_water = mean(water_ppm))
Batches made from Kestrel Industrial’s lots average about 320 ppm of water, against roughly 180 to 205 ppm for everyone else. B18 used lot L26-006, a Kestrel lot. Mystery solved: the raw material arrived wet. Notice that select() can rename as it goes: water_ppm = value.

Making decisions
An if statement runs code only when a condition is true:

purity &lt;- 98.9
if (purity &gt;= 98.5) {
  "on spec"
} else {
  "off spec"
}
#&gt; [1] "on spec"

The condition goes in parentheses and must be a single TRUE or FALSE. If it’s TRUE, the code in the first pair of braces runs. Otherwise the else part runs. (else is optional: without it, nothing happens when the condition is FALSE.)
For more than two outcomes, chain conditions with else if. R checks them from top to bottom and runs the first branch whose condition is TRUE:

pH &lt;- 8.2
if (pH &lt; 6.5) {
  "too acidic: add base"
} else if (pH &lt;= 7.5) {
  "in range"
} else {
  "too basic: add acid"
}
#&gt; [1] "too basic: add acid"

Try different pH values here, including exactly 6.5 and exactly 7.5. Which branch do they land in?








Your turn. Fill in the condition so the code says "on spec" when the purity is at least 98.5%.









Compare purity with 98.5 using &gt;=.


purity &lt;- 98.9
if (purity &gt;= 98.5) {
  "on spec"
} else {
  "off spec"
}

Writing your own functions
When you find yourself doing the same calculation again and again, wrap it in a function. Here’s the ideal gas law, solved for the number of moles:

moles &lt;- function(P_Pa, V_m3, T_K) {
  P_Pa * V_m3 / (8.314 * T_K)
}
moles(101325, 0.05, 298.15)
#&gt; [1] 2.043816
moles(P_Pa = 200000, V_m3 = 0.05, T_K = 298.15)
#&gt; [1] 4.034179

A function has a name (moles), arguments in the parentheses after function, and a body in braces. The value of the last line of the body is what the function gives back. (You can also leave early with return(value).)
Because the body uses vectorized arithmetic, your function works on whole vectors for free. Here’s a tank at three different temperatures:

moles(101325, 0.05, c(273.15, 298.15, 373.15))
#&gt; [1] 2.230876 2.043816 1.633026

Your turn. Analytical chemists judge how precise their replicate measurements are with the relative standard deviation (RSD): the standard deviation as a percentage of the mean. Write rsd().









The body is one line: sd(x) divided by mean(x), times 100.


rsd &lt;- function(x) {
  100 * sd(x) / mean(x)
}
rsd(c(10.2, 10.5, 9.9, 10.1, 10.3))
About 2.2%: the replicates agree within a couple of percent.




Your turn. Complete classify_temp(): below 170 °C the reactor is "too cold", from 170 up to and including 190 it’s "in range", and above 190 it’s "too hot".









By the time R reaches else if, it already knows the temperature is at least 170. So the only thing left to check is whether it’s at most 190.


classify_temp &lt;- function(temp_C) {
  if (temp_C &lt; 170) {
    "too cold"
  } else if (temp_C &lt;= 190) {
    "in range"
  } else {
    "too hot"
  }
}
classify_temp(182)

Defaults and scope
Give an argument a default with =, and you can leave it out when you call the function. The gas constant is a perfect example: it’s almost always 8.314 J/(mol·K), but sometimes you’d rather work in liters and atmospheres:

moles &lt;- function(P, V, T_K, R = 8.314) {
  P * V / (R * T_K)
}
moles(101325, 0.05, 298.15)                   # Pa and m³, the default R
#&gt; [1] 2.043816
moles(1, 50, 298.15, R = 0.082057)            # atm and liters
#&gt; [1] 2.043711

Same answer, about 2.04 mol, in two unit systems.

What happens inside stays inside
Objects created inside a function exist only while it runs. This is called scope, and it means a function can’t accidentally overwrite your own objects:

f &lt;- function() {
  inside &lt;- 42
  inside * 2
}
f()
#&gt; [1] 84
exists("inside")
#&gt; [1] FALSE



Failing clearly
A good function stops with a helpful message when it’s given the wrong kind of input. stop() ends the function with your own error message:

rsd &lt;- function(x) {
  if (!is.numeric(x)) stop("rsd() needs numbers, but got ", class(x))
  100 * sd(x) / mean(x)
}
rsd(c(10.2, 10.5, 9.9))
#&gt; [1] 2.941176
rsd("ten")
#&gt; Error in `rsd()`:
#&gt; ! rsd() needs numbers, but got character

Your turn. Complete to_kelvin(). By default it converts from Celsius. With scale = "F" it converts from Fahrenheit: subtract 32, multiply by 5/9, then add 273.15.









Translate the recipe directly: (temp - 32) * 5 / 9 + 273.15.


to_kelvin &lt;- function(temp, scale = "C") {
  if (scale == "C") {
    temp + 273.15
  } else {
    (temp - 32) * 5 / 9 + 273.15
  }
}
to_kelvin(25)
to_kelvin(212, scale = "F")
212 °F is the boiling point of water, 373.15 K.

Challenge round
Challenge 1 · Better or worse than its catalyst? For each batch, compute vs_catalyst: its yield minus the mean yield of its own catalyst. Keep only the columns batch_id, catalyst, yield_pct and vs_catalyst.









Group by catalyst, then use mutate() instead of summarise(), because you want to keep every batch. Inside a grouped mutate(), mean(yield_pct) is the mean of each batch’s own group.


group_by(catalyst) |&gt; mutate(vs_catalyst = yield_pct - mean(yield_pct)) |&gt; ungroup() |&gt; select(...)


batches |&gt;
  group_by(catalyst) |&gt;
  mutate(vs_catalyst = yield_pct - mean(yield_pct)) |&gt;
  ungroup() |&gt;
  select(batch_id, catalyst, yield_pct, vs_catalyst)
Within each catalyst, the new column adds up to zero: the deviations from Lesson 2, computed group by group.




Challenge 2 · The worst batch for each catalyst. Process engineers learn a lot from their worst runs. For each catalyst, find the batch with the lowest yield, and show its batch_id, catalyst and yield_pct.









filter() works within groups too. After group_by(catalyst), the condition yield_pct == min(yield_pct) keeps the lowest yield in each group.


batches |&gt; group_by(catalyst) |&gt; filter(yield_pct == min(yield_pct)) |&gt; select(______)


batches |&gt;
  group_by(catalyst) |&gt;
  filter(yield_pct == min(yield_pct)) |&gt;
  select(batch_id, catalyst, yield_pct)
slice_min(yield_pct, n = 1) does the same job as that filter(). B10 and B40 are the really bad ones, at 31% and 37%. A good next step would be to check their alarm counts!

Many columns at once: across()
To compute the mean of four columns, you could write four lines inside summarise(). across() does it in one: give it the columns, then the function.

batches |&gt;
  group_by(reactor) |&gt;
  summarise(across(c(temp_C, pressure_bar, residence_min, yield_pct), mean))
#&gt; # A tibble: 2 × 5
#&gt;   reactor temp_C pressure_bar residence_min yield_pct
#&gt;   &lt;chr&gt;    &lt;dbl&gt;        &lt;dbl&gt;         &lt;dbl&gt;     &lt;dbl&gt;
#&gt; 1 R1         NA          4.90          47.4      60.9
#&gt; 2 R2        179.         5.08          42.2      54.4

The temperature is NA because two batches have no temperature. To pass na.rm = TRUE, write the function as an anonymous function, with the \(x) shorthand from Module 0:

batches |&gt;
  group_by(reactor) |&gt;
  summarise(across(c(temp_C, pressure_bar, residence_min, yield_pct), \(x) mean(x, na.rm = TRUE)))
#&gt; # A tibble: 2 × 5
#&gt;   reactor temp_C pressure_bar residence_min yield_pct
#&gt;   &lt;chr&gt;    &lt;dbl&gt;        &lt;dbl&gt;         &lt;dbl&gt;     &lt;dbl&gt;
#&gt; 1 R1        180.         4.90          47.4      60.9
#&gt; 2 R2        179.         5.08          42.2      54.4

R1 averages 61% yield, against 54% for R2, with longer residence times. That’s a lead worth following up!
You can pick the columns with the select() helpers you already know, like where(is.numeric) or ends_with("_pct"), and apply several functions at once by giving across() a named list. The new columns are named column_function:

batches |&gt;
  summarise(across(c(yield_pct, residence_min), list(mean = mean, sd = sd)))
#&gt;   yield_pct_mean yield_pct_sd residence_min_mean residence_min_sd
#&gt; 1        57.6475     9.749477              44.75         11.23463

across() works inside mutate() too. Here it rounds every numeric column in one go:

batches |&gt;
  mutate(across(where(is.numeric), \(x) round(x))) |&gt;
  select(batch_id, temp_C:yield_pct) |&gt;
  head(3)
#&gt;   batch_id temp_C pressure_bar residence_min alarms yield_pct
#&gt; 1      B01    185            4            47      1        70
#&gt; 2      B02    176            5            38      1        54
#&gt; 3      B03    178            5            32      1        49











Your turn. For each catalyst, compute the median of temp_C, residence_min and yield_pct, ignoring missing values. (batches and sensor are ready in every exercise.)









The first argument of across() lists the columns: c(temp_C, residence_min, yield_pct).


batches |&gt;
  group_by(catalyst) |&gt;
  summarise(across(c(temp_C, residence_min, yield_pct), \(x) median(x, na.rm = TRUE)))




Your turn. Over all the batches, compute the minimum and maximum of yield_pct and pressure_bar with a single across(). The new columns should be called yield_pct_min, yield_pct_max, pressure_bar_min and pressure_bar_max.









Give across() a named list of functions: list(min = min, max = max).


batches |&gt;
  summarise(across(c(yield_pct, pressure_bar), list(min = min, max = max)))

Running totals and records
The cumulative functions build up a value row by row: cumsum() for a running total, cummax() and cummin() for the largest and smallest so far, and cummean() for the running mean.

alarms &lt;- c(1, 0, 3, 1, 2)
cumsum(alarms)
#&gt; [1] 1 1 4 5 7
cummax(alarms)
#&gt; [1] 1 1 3 3 3

Here’s a running count of alarms on each reactor over the campaign. The batches have to be in time order first:

batches |&gt;
  arrange(start_time) |&gt;
  group_by(reactor) |&gt;
  mutate(cum_alarms = cumsum(alarms)) |&gt;
  select(batch_id, reactor, alarms, cum_alarms) |&gt;
  filter(reactor == "R1") |&gt;
  tail(6)
#&gt; # A tibble: 6 × 4
#&gt; # Groups:   reactor [1]
#&gt;   batch_id reactor alarms cum_alarms
#&gt;   &lt;chr&gt;    &lt;chr&gt;    &lt;int&gt;      &lt;int&gt;
#&gt; 1 B29      R1           1         20
#&gt; 2 B31      R1          NA         NA
#&gt; 3 B33      R1           1         NA
#&gt; 4 B35      R1           2         NA
#&gt; 5 B37      R1           0         NA
#&gt; 6 B39      R1           1         NA

From batch B31 onwards the total is NA, because B31’s alarm log was lost, and once a running total meets an NA, it stays NA. If you’re happy to count the missing value as zero, coalesce(alarms, 0) replaces each NA with 0. (Is zero a fair guess? Lesson 12 is all about questions like that.)
Your turn. Compute each reactor’s running alarm total, counting the missing alarm log as 0, and return each reactor’s total at the end of the campaign.









Inside cumsum(), replace the missing values first: coalesce(alarms, 0).


batches |&gt;
  arrange(start_time) |&gt;
  group_by(reactor) |&gt;
  mutate(cum_alarms = cumsum(coalesce(alarms, 0))) |&gt;
  summarise(total = last(cum_alarms))
24 alarms on R1 and 25 on R2. last() takes the last value in each group.

Grouped calculations that keep every row
group_by() followed by summarise() collapses each group to one row. Followed by mutate() or filter(), it works within groups but keeps every row. That’s how you compare each batch with its own group, as in Module 0’s last challenge. Here, each batch’s yield as a z-score within its catalyst:

batches |&gt;
  group_by(catalyst) |&gt;
  mutate(z_in_catalyst = (yield_pct - mean(yield_pct)) / sd(yield_pct)) |&gt;
  ungroup() |&gt;
  select(batch_id, catalyst, yield_pct, z_in_catalyst) |&gt;
  head(4)
#&gt; # A tibble: 4 × 4
#&gt;   batch_id catalyst yield_pct z_in_catalyst
#&gt;   &lt;chr&gt;    &lt;chr&gt;        &lt;dbl&gt;         &lt;dbl&gt;
#&gt; 1 B01      B             70.2         1.11 
#&gt; 2 B02      D             53.7        -0.948
#&gt; 3 B03      C             49.2        -0.549
#&gt; 4 B04      B             65.3         0.736

And a grouped filter() keeps rows by a condition that uses the group’s own summaries, like “batches more than 10 points below their catalyst’s mean yield”:

batches |&gt;
  group_by(catalyst) |&gt;
  filter(yield_pct &lt; mean(yield_pct) - 10) |&gt;
  select(batch_id, catalyst, yield_pct)
#&gt; # A tibble: 3 × 3
#&gt; # Groups:   catalyst [2]
#&gt;   batch_id catalyst yield_pct
#&gt;   &lt;chr&gt;    &lt;chr&gt;        &lt;dbl&gt;
#&gt; 1 B10      B             31.1
#&gt; 2 B14      B             33.8
#&gt; 3 B40      C             36.8

Your turn. How many batches have a yield above the mean yield of their own catalyst?









Inside a grouped filter(), mean(yield_pct) is the mean of each batch’s own catalyst: yield_pct &gt; mean(yield_pct).


batches |&gt;
  group_by(catalyst) |&gt;
  filter(yield_pct &gt; mean(yield_pct)) |&gt;
  ungroup() |&gt;
  nrow()

Challenge round
Challenge 1 · When did each reactor reach temperature? Find the first time stamp at which each reactor’s temperature rose to 150 °C or more, coming from below 150 °C at the previous reading. Drop the glitch first.









You want rows where the temperature is now at least 150 and the previous reading was below 150. Combine the two with a comma inside filter().


filter(temp_C &gt;= 150, lag(temp_C) &lt; 150)


sensor |&gt;
  filter(temp_C &lt; 500) |&gt;
  group_by(reactor) |&gt;
  filter(temp_C &gt;= 150, lag(temp_C) &lt; 150) |&gt;
  slice_head(n = 1) |&gt;
  select(timestamp, reactor, temp_C)
R2 reached temperature at 07:35, and R1 at 14:05. Without the glitch filter, R1 would seem to “reach temperature” at 03:15 in the morning, in the middle of nowhere!




Challenge 2 · Record batches. Going through the campaign in time order, which batches set a new yield record, beating every batch before them? Return their batch_ids.









A batch sets a record when its yield equals the highest yield so far, which is cummax(yield_pct).


batches |&gt;
  arrange(start_time) |&gt;
  filter(yield_pct == cummax(yield_pct)) |&gt;
  pull(batch_id)
B02, then B01, B07 and B11. B11’s 74.8% stood for the rest of the campaign. pull() takes a single column out as a vector.

Challenge round
