Challenge 2 · SAS’s baseline. A SAS colleague fitted the yield on the catalyst with PROC GLM; CLASS catalyst; MODEL yield_pct = catalyst / SOLUTION;, which uses the last catalyst, D, as the baseline. Their coefficient for catalyst A is −0.49. Reproduce it in R by making D the reference level.









The reference level is given as text: ref = "D".


batches |&gt;
  mutate(catalyst = relevel(factor(catalyst), ref = "D")) |&gt;
  lm(yield_pct ~ catalyst, data = _) |&gt;
  coef()
Catalyst A’s coefficient is −0.49: its batches averaged 0.49 percentage points less than catalyst D’s. With R’s default baseline, A, the same model says that D is 0.49 more than A. Same model, same fit, different point of view. (The _ is the native pipe’s placeholder: it passes the data frame to the data argument.)

Leverage
The diagonal entries h_{ii} are special. Each one says how much observation i’s own response pulls on its own fitted value, and it’s called that observation’s leverage. In simple regression, it has a formula you can read:

h_{ii} = \frac{1}{n} + \frac{(x_i - \bar{x})^2}{S_{xx}} .

A batch with an average residence time has the smallest possible leverage, 1/n. The further its x is from the average, the more leverage it has, like a person sitting at the end of a seesaw. Leverages always lie between 1/n and 1, and since they add up to p, they average p/n, here 2/40 = 0.05. A common rule of thumb flags any observation with more than twice the average, 2p/n.
hatvalues() returns the leverages of any model:

batches |&gt;
  mutate(leverage = hatvalues(fit)) |&gt;
  arrange(desc(leverage)) |&gt;
  select(batch_id, residence_min, leverage) |&gt;
  head(5)
#&gt; # A tibble: 5 × 3
#&gt;   batch_id residence_min leverage
#&gt;   &lt;chr&gt;            &lt;dbl&gt;    &lt;dbl&gt;
#&gt; 1 B40               15.9    0.194
#&gt; 2 B29               71.7    0.173
#&gt; 3 B10               21      0.140
#&gt; 4 B33               65.4    0.112
#&gt; 5 B07               64      0.100

Five batches pass 2p/n = 0.1, B07 only just: the two shortest runs and the three longest. High leverage isn’t bad in itself. It means those batches have a big say in where the line goes, so if one of them had a typo in its yield, the line would follow it. Lesson 10 is about telling a harmless high-leverage point from a harmful one.
Your turn. Which batch has the highest leverage? Use slice_max().









hatvalues(fit) returns the leverages, in row order.


batches |&gt;
  mutate(leverage = hatvalues(fit)) |&gt;
  slice_max(leverage) |&gt;
  select(batch_id, residence_min, leverage)
B40, the shortest run at 15.9 minutes, with a leverage of 0.19: almost four times the average. The batch with the lowest leverage, B19, ran for 44.9 minutes, right next to the average, and its leverage is 1/40 = 0.025.

Leverage
Leverage is the diagonal of the hat matrix, h_{ii}, from Lesson 4. It measures how far a run’s predictor values are from the rest, taking all the predictors into account at once, including the curved and interaction terms. The leverages average p/n, where p counts the coefficients. This model has p = 10 and n = 120, so the average is 0.083, and the usual flag, twice the average, is 2p/n = 0.17.

study |&gt;
  mutate(leverage = hatvalues(fit)) |&gt;
  arrange(desc(leverage)) |&gt;
  select(run_id, catalyst, temp_C, residence_min, leverage) |&gt;
  head(5)
#&gt; # A tibble: 5 × 5
#&gt;   run_id catalyst temp_C residence_min leverage
#&gt;   &lt;chr&gt;  &lt;chr&gt;     &lt;dbl&gt;         &lt;dbl&gt;    &lt;dbl&gt;
#&gt; 1 P066   B          158.          38.6    0.386
#&gt; 2 P019   A          206.          75.8    0.181
#&gt; 3 P105   C          206.          71.9    0.154
#&gt; 4 P043   C          206.          63.9    0.148
#&gt; 5 P115   C          180.          95      0.148

P066 stands out, with a leverage of 0.39, more than twice the flag: a catalyst B run at 158 °C is unlike anything else in the study. Its temperature alone gives it a long lever, and the squared term lengthens it. Next come runs at the corners of the design, like P019 at 206 °C with 76 minutes of residence time. They’re unusual, but legitimately so: the study was designed to cover those corners. And P115, the deliberate 95-minute trial, has a leverage of 0.15.
Your turn. Which runs pass the 2p/n flag?









The flag is twice the average leverage: 2 * p / n.


p &lt;- length(coef(fit))
n &lt;- nrow(study)
study |&gt;
  mutate(leverage = hatvalues(fit)) |&gt;
  filter(leverage &gt; 2 * p / n) |&gt;
  select(run_id, temp_C, residence_min, leverage)
Two runs: P066 (0.39) and P019 (0.18). High leverage is a reason to look closely at a run, not a reason to remove it. P019 is a legitimate corner of the design. P066 is the one with a story to check.
